 "transcript": {
  "answer": "In September 2026 the advertised standard variable rate for owner-occupier home loans was **about 4.4 percentage points above the cash rate**.\n\n**September 2026:**\n- Banks' advertised standard variable rate (owner-occupier): **8.77%**\n- Cash rate target (monthly average): **4.36%**\n- **Gap: 4.41 percentage points**\n\n**The gap has barely changed.** Banks have passed rate moves on almost exactly, so the gap has stayed at about 4.4 points:\n\n| Month | Standard variable | Cash rate (avg) | Gap |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Jun 2025 | 8.27% | 3.85% | 4.42 pts |\n| Dec 2025 | 8.02% | 3.60% | 4.42 pts |\n| Sep 2026 | 8.77% | 4.36% | 4.41 pts |\n\nSome months in between show bigger or smaller gaps, such as 4.56 points in March 2026 and 4.42 in April. That's because the cash rate figure is a monthly average, so when rates were rising in 2026 it trailed the advertised rate, which moved in a single step. The usual gap is still about 4.4 points.\n\n**Hardly anyone pays the standard variable rate.** The advertised *discounted* variable rate was 6.80% in September 2026, only **2.44 points** above the cash rate. Rates actually paid on new loans are usually lower again (RBA table F6).\n\nSources: RBA tables F1.1 (cash rate) and F5 (advertised lending rates).",
